---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'About spousalsupportcalc.com';
const description = 'About the team behind spousalsupportcalc.com. Free family-law calculators and plain-English guides reviewed by the editorial team at The Click Lab.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>About spousalsupportcalc.com</h1>
  <p>spousalsupportcalc.com is a free family-law resource maintained by The Click Lab. Our calculator and guides help separating spouses understand how spousal support (alimony) is typically calculated, what a realistic range looks like before they've spoken with an attorney, and which factors are likely to move a judge's actual order up or down from any formula-based estimate. The Site is built for people who are early in the separation process and want a plain-English starting point &mdash; not a substitute for legal advice.</p>

  <h2>What we do</h2>
  <p>We translate family-law concepts into plain-English calculators and explanations. Every page is reviewed by our <a href="/editorial/">editorial team</a> with citations to state statutes and reported case law. The calculator applies a default formula-based approach similar to the guidelines many states use for temporary support, then layers in state-specific variations and the discretionary factors &mdash; length of marriage, earning capacity, standard of living, and others &mdash; that routinely change the number a judge actually orders. The goal isn't to predict your exact outcome; it's to give you a defensible starting range and to explain, in plain language, why the real number might land somewhere else.</p>

  <h2>Who this calculator is for</h2>
  <p>Most visitors fall into one of a few groups: people considering separation who want to understand roughly what's at stake before they talk to a lawyer; self-represented (pro se) litigants preparing for a hearing who need a sanity check on a proposed number; and family-law paraprofessionals &mdash; mediators, paralegals, financial planners working with divorcing clients &mdash; who want a quick reference point before pulling the state-specific guideline. We built the Site around these use cases rather than around exhaustive legal research, which is why every guide leads with a plain-English answer before it gets into statutory detail.</p>

  <h2>How the estimate is built</h2>
  <p>The full mechanics &mdash; the default formula, worked examples, and state-by-state variations &mdash; are documented on our <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>. At a high level: the calculator starts from a formula common to many states' temporary-support guidelines, then swaps in a state-specific formula when you select a state we've modeled directly (currently California, Massachusetts, Pennsylvania, Texas, and Illinois, with more states being added over time). For states without a codified formula, the estimate defaults to the general formula and flags that the real outcome depends more heavily on judicial discretion.</p>

  <h2>What we don't do</h2>
  <p>We don't provide legal advice, and nothing on this Site creates an attorney-client relationship. Spousal support outcomes depend heavily on jurisdiction, the specific facts of a marriage, and the judge assigned to the case &mdash; three variables no calculator can fully account for. We also don't evaluate individual cases, review specific settlement offers, or recommend particular attorneys or mediators. For your specific situation, consult a licensed family-law attorney in your state.</p>

  <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
  <p>Every guide and reference page is written from primary sources &mdash; state family codes, appellate decisions, and state judicial-council guidelines &mdash; and reviewed before publication. We don't publish thin content: each page is meant to answer a specific question a non-lawyer would actually ask, with enough detail to be useful. Our full editorial workflow, contributor credentials, and sourcing policy are on the <a href="/editorial/">editorial team page</a>.</p>

  <h2>How we make money</h2>
  <p>Display advertising via Google AdSense. We don't sell reader data or accept paid placements, and no attorney, mediator, or divorce-tech company pays for placement or a favorable mention anywhere on the Site. See our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for details on data handling.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions, corrections, or feedback: see our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
